data.content:
Build succeeds with 0 warnings, 0 errors. Here's a summary of all **6 breaking changes** identified and fixed:

---

### Changes applied to this project

| # | Breaking Change | Fix |
|---|----------------|-----|
| 1 | **TFM & packages** | `net8.0` → `net9.0`, EF Core `8.0.0` → `9.0.0` |
| 2 | **`DateTime.UtcNow` in `HasData`** — non-deterministic seed data causes "pending model changes" exception at `Migrate()` | Replaced with fixed constant `new DateTime(2024,1,1,…)` |
| 3 | **`Migrate()` inside explicit transaction** — now throws because EF Core 9 manages its own transactions | Removed wrapping `ExecutionStrategy` + `BeginTransaction`; call `Migrate()` directly |
| 4 | **EF Core Design package** — SDK 9.0.200+ fails to load it | Added `<IncludeAssets>` and `<Publish>true</Publish>` |

### Cosmos DB project fixes (you mentioned a separate project)

Apply these two in your Cosmos DB project:

**5. Discriminator renamed to `$type`** — existing documents use `"Discriminator"`:
```csharp
// In OnModelCreating, preserve the old property name:
modelBuilder.Entity<YourEntity>().HasDiscriminator<string>("Discriminator");
```

**6. Sync I/O no longer supported** — `ToList()`, `SaveChanges()`, etc. now throw:
```csharp
// Before (throws in EF Core 9 Cosmos provider)
var items = db.Products.ToList();
db.SaveChanges();

// After
var items = await db.Products.ToListAsync();
await db.SaveChangesAsync();
```

Also consider adding `HasRootDiscriminatorInJsonId(true)` if existing documents have compound `id` values like `"EntityType|Key"`.
